\section{Globbing}

\begin{note}
After variable and command expansion, the shell expands glob patterns
such as \lstinline{*} in any word that is not quoted.
Single quotes \lstinline{'}, double quotes \lstinline{"}, and backslashes
all suppress glob expansion.
A backslash removes the special meaning of the character that follows it,
so a backslash followed by a space (a ``backslash-space'') keeps the space
inside a single argument instead of splitting on it.
\end{note}
